# PDW Paging Decoder

PDW is a native Windows decoder for POCSAG, FLEX, ACARS, MOBITEX, and
ERMES signals. It accepts input from a supported serial interface or a Windows
audio capture device and provides monitoring, filtering, logging, alerts, and
statistics.

PDW was originally developed with Microsoft Visual C++ 6.0. The active build
uses CMake and the Microsoft Visual C++ toolchain. The decoder remains a
32-bit application while its legacy slicer and serial integration are being
preserved.

## Development status

Version 4.1.0 Beta is the first application-focused modernization of the
open-source release. Decoder behaviour is intentionally
being preserved while the Windows build, safety, configuration, and interface
are updated in small, testable changes. See [CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md) and
[docs/MODERNIZATION.md](docs/MODERNIZATION.md).

## Build on Windows

Prerequisites:

- Windows 10 or Windows 11
- Visual Studio 2022 with **Desktop development with C++**
- A current Windows SDK
- CMake 3.20 or newer
- Perl 5 (Strawberry Perl or ActivePerl)
- PowerShell 7 or Windows PowerShell 5.1

From a Developer PowerShell for Visual Studio:

```powershell
.\scripts\build-dependencies.ps1
cmake -S . -B build -G "Visual Studio 17 2022" -A Win32
cmake --build build --config Release --target PDW PDWNotificationTests
ctest --test-dir build -C Release --output-on-failure
```

The dependency script downloads official source archives, verifies their
SHA-256 checksums, and builds the pinned Win32 static dependencies under
`out\dependencies\x86`. It currently pins OpenSSL 3.5.7, curl 8.21.0, and
libssh2 1.11.1. The executable is written to
`build\Release\PDW v4.1.0 Beta.exe`. GitHub
Actions performs the same dependency and Win32 Release build for every push
and pull request to `master`.

## Name separate PDW instances

PDW supports an optional instance name in `pdw.ini`. This is useful when
separate PDW folders monitor different receivers or paging systems.

```ini
[PDW]
WindowTitle=North Dispatch
```

After PDW restarts, the main window and system-tray tooltip display
`North Dispatch - PDW v4.1.0 Beta`. The value is retained when PDW saves its
settings. PDW still permits only one running instance per application folder;
use a separate folder and configuration for each concurrent instance.

## Start PDW with Windows

Open **Settings > Appearance and startup** and select **Start PDW with
Windows** to launch the current PDW installation automatically when you sign
in. Clear the same checkbox to disable it. Automatic launches wait five
seconds to allow Windows and connected devices to settle; opening PDW
manually is not delayed.

## Publish files by FTP, FTPS, or SFTP

Open **Settings > Connections and automation > File transfer** to select one or more files for continuous
upload to a hosting account. PDW supports FTP, explicit FTPS, implicit FTPS,
and password-authenticated SFTP. Server details, the remote folder, selected
files, and an interval in seconds can be saved. Transfers run in the
background, and `FileTransfer.log` records their results.

The hosting password is stored in Windows Credential Manager rather than
`pdw.ini`. FTPS verifies the server certificate against the Windows trust
store. SFTP requires the hosting provider's SHA-256 SSH host-key fingerprint,
which protects against connecting to an impersonating server. Classic FTP is
not encrypted and should be used only when the hosting provider offers no
secure option. See [docs/FTP_UPLOAD.md](docs/FTP_UPLOAD.md) for setup and
security details.

## Send filtered messages through Apprise

Open **Settings > Connections and automation > Push notifications** to configure an authenticated HTTPS Apprise API and
select **Enable Apprise**. Every message that reaches PDW's filtered-message
output is queued in the background; rejected, duplicate, monitor-only, and
unfiltered messages are not sent. SMTP/email remains independently enabled
under its existing menu option.

The endpoint, destination URLs, and API credentials are stored in Windows
Credential Manager and masked in the dialog. PDW does not bundle Python or
silently select a hosted notification service. See
[docs/APPRISE_NOTIFICATIONS.md](docs/APPRISE_NOTIFICATIONS.md) for architecture,
server hardening, privacy behaviour, and ntfy/Pushover phone-test steps.

## Radio input, signal recording, and replay

Open **Settings > Decoder and input > Radio and replay**. The original local
audio/serial choice remains available and still tries WinMM first; WASAPI is an
automatic fallback when that legacy audio format cannot be opened. The same
dialog can select a reconnecting `rtl_tcp` receiver or direct RTL-SDR USB.
The distribution keeps its standard RTL2832U/V3/V4/V4L support files together
under `Receivers`, lists connected devices, and can import another compatible
32-bit receiver DLL package without removing the side-by-side legacy fallback.

Use **Record** to save the normalized signal as 16-bit mono WAV or SigMF
`rf32_le`. Use **Replay** to send a WAV or SigMF recording through PDW's normal
protocol routines. Replay temporarily suspends the current live source and
restores that exact audio, radio, or serial source when replay finishes or is
stopped. Existing `.rec` recording/playback remains unchanged.

The dialog also provides adjustable NFM bandwidth, live waveform and quality
history, signal/noise/clipping/eye metrics, FLEX phase error totals, and a
recording calibration action. Calibration compares all custom threshold,
centering and resync combinations and applies a suggestion only after user
confirmation; the original receiver presets are retained.

## Publish decoded messages to a website or webhook

Open **Settings > Connections and automation > Publish to web**. Publishing is
off by default and cannot be enabled until the operator acknowledges that they
must check permissions, privacy obligations, radio laws, and publication laws
in their own country or jurisdiction.

PDW can maintain `messages.json`, `messages.jsonl`, `messages.rss`,
`messages.atom`, and a responsive `index.html` in a chosen folder. Those files
can be uploaded with PDW's existing FTP/FTPS/SFTP feature. PDW can also deliver
each event to a generic HTTPS JSON webhook with optional bearer-token and HMAC
authentication stored in Windows Credential Manager. Address masking, source
aliasing, and message omission modify only the published copy; the decoded
message shown and logged by PDW is not changed. Delivery is asynchronous, can
be paused or rate-limited, retries with backoff, and retains repeated failures
under `PublishQueue\DeadLetter`.

See [docs/PUBLISHING.md](docs/PUBLISHING.md) and
[docs/SIGNAL_SOURCES.md](docs/SIGNAL_SOURCES.md) for setup and safety details.

## Optional data outputs

Open **Settings > Connections and automation > Data outputs** to route the same
immutable decoded-event copy to MQTT, a local SQLite database, MySQL through a
Windows ODBC DSN, a loopback-first read-only Telnet JSON stream, or native
Windows notifications. These adapters are disabled by default and do not
replace email, Apprise, FTP, website files, webhooks, or any legacy decoder
path. Filtered-only routing, address masking, and message omission are the
safe defaults, and enabling the group requires the same country/jurisdiction
responsibility acknowledgement.

MQTT uses the pinned libcurl transport (QoS 0, no retain), SQLite uses the
Windows system component with prepared statements, MySQL relies on the
operator's current ODBC driver and DSN TLS policy, and remote Telnet requires
an explicit unencrypted-access opt-in. Passwords stay in Windows Credential
Manager. See [docs/DATA_OUTPUTS.md](docs/DATA_OUTPUTS.md) for setup, limitations,
and test behaviour.

## Repository layout

- `PDW.cpp` and protocol-specific `.cpp` files contain the application and
  decoders.
- `Headers/` contains application headers and the canonical version metadata.
- `utils/` contains serial, notification, SMTP, and supporting code.
- `GFX/` and `Rsrc.rc` contain the native Windows resources.
- `scripts/` contains the reproducible third-party dependency build.
- `docs/` contains operator and modernization guidance.

Third-party component versions, source locations, checksums, and licenses are
listed in [THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md](THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md).

